<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Fribourg</h1><p class="st-meta">1478 CE – 1798 CE · capital Fribourg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A bilingual city-state founded by the Zähringen dukes on the Saane, which shook off Savoy in 1477 and joined the Swiss Confederacy in 1481.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berthold-iv-of-zahringen">Berthold IV of Zähringen</a> founded Fribourg in 1157 on a rocky bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarine">Saane</a>. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-kyburg">Kyburgs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and in 1452 to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy</a>. After fighting beside the Swiss in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundian-wars">Burgundian Wars</a>, it was released by Savoy and became a free imperial city in 1478, joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederate-states-of-america">Confederacy</a> in 1481 together with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-solothurn">Solothurn</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A small circle of patrician families ruled the city and its rural territory, which straddled the language border between French and German. Fribourg prospered from cloth and leather in the 15th century. It remained Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and became a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>: Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-canisius">Peter Canisius</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college of Saint Michael there in 1582.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The patrician regime fell to the French invasion of 1798, and Fribourg became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. Restored as a canton in 1803, it remains a bilingual Swiss canton.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/canton-of-fribourg.jpg" alt="Canton of Fribourg" width="320" height="268"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fribourg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1478 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fribourg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fribourg, Freiburg im Üechtland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1638&amp;focus=canton-of-fribourg" aria-label="Canton of Fribourg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4848 533 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Canton of Fribourg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5168 789L5186 782M5168 789L5188 772M5168 789L5167 795"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 782h0M5188 772h0M5167 795h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5168 789h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5168 789h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Fribourg. <a href="/atlas?year=1638&amp;focus=canton-of-fribourg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
